# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
A(('h1', S1))
A(('p', 'By the end of this study, you should be able to:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Follow the two short comparisons in the order each gospel gives them: a piece of new cloth set on an old garment, and new wine put into old bottles, with Luke’s added saying about old wine.',
    'Mark where each comparison stands: after a feast at a tax collector’s house and a question about fasting, and after Jesus’ words about the bridegroom.',
    'See that Jesus gives no explanation of either comparison, and mark every part of the stories He leaves unnamed.',
    'State what the comparisons say in their own words, without assigning an identity to the cloth, the garment, the wine, the bottles or the bridegroom.',
    'Set Matthew, Mark and Luke side by side and record exactly where their wording differs, without merging the three accounts.',
    'Keep this passage apart from the other passages in which Jesus is asked about eating and drinking, and place it in Jesus’ earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap.',
    'State a principled lesson for today and say plainly why following, feasting and putting in are not steps of salvation.',
]))
A(('call', 'Study Goal',
   'To read Matthew 9:16–17, Mark 2:21–22 and Luke 5:36–39 exactly as they stand, to mark what Jesus does not identify, and to state what the comparisons say without adding a referent, a symbol or a doctrine the text does not supply.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene in Matthew. Matthew records the call of a man at his work: “he saw a man, named Matthew, sitting at the receipt of custom: and he saith unto him, Follow me. And he arose, and followed him” (9:9). Then “as Jesus sat at meat in the house, behold, many publicans and sinners came and sat down with him and his disciples” (9:10). “And when the Pharisees saw it, they said unto his disciples, Why eateth your Master with publicans and sinners?” (9:11).'))
A(('p', '2.2 Jesus’ answer to the Pharisees. “But when Jesus heard that, he said unto them, They that be whole need not a physician, but they that are sick” (9:12). He continues, “But go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance” (9:13). That answer is given to the Pharisees’ question about eating with publicans and sinners.'))
A(('call', 'The Question Behind the Comparisons',
   'A second question follows. “Then came to him the disciples of John, saying, Why do we and the Pharisees fast oft, but thy disciples fast not?” (Matthew 9:14). The comparisons come after Jesus has answered it with the words about the children of the bridechamber and the bridegroom. The text does not say that either comparison is an answer to the question. It records the question, the answer about the bridegroom, and then the comparisons.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The scene in Mark and in Luke. Mark places the same sequence by the sea. “And he went forth again by the sea side; and all the multitude resorted unto him, and he taught them” (2:13). The man at the receipt of custom is named Levi the son of Alphaeus (2:14). The feast is “in his house” (2:15), and “the scribes and Pharisees” put the question to the disciples (2:16). The question about fasting comes from another quarter: “the disciples of John and of the Pharisees used to fast: and they come and say unto him, Why do the disciples of John and of the Pharisees fast, but thy disciples fast not?” (2:18). Luke names the man Levi (5:27), says “Levi made him a great feast in his own house” (5:29), and records the murmuring of “their scribes and Pharisees” (5:30). The question about fasting is put by “they” (5:33), which follows the murmuring of 5:30 in Luke’s order.'))
A(('p', '2.4 Where the comparisons stand. In each gospel the comparisons come right after the saying about the bridegroom. In Matthew, the saying is “Can the children of the bridechamber mourn, as long as the bridegroom is with them? but the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken from them, and then shall they fast” (9:15). Mark and Luke have the same saying with “fast” in place of “mourn” (Mark 2:19–20; Luke 5:34–35). Matthew has no joining word before the cloth (9:16). Mark has “No man also seweth” (2:21), and Luke has “And he spake also a parable unto them” (5:36). Luke is the only one of the three that calls the saying a parable.'))
A(('p', '2.5 What follows in each gospel. After the comparisons Matthew goes at once to another scene: “While he spake these things unto them, behold, there came a certain ruler” (9:18). Mark goes to a Sabbath in the corn fields (2:23). Luke adds one more saying about old wine (5:39) and then turns to another Sabbath (6:1). None of the three records a reply to the comparisons.'))
A(('p', '2.6 The prophetic context. The three gospels stand in the ministry in which the Kingdom was preached to Israel. “From that time Jesus began to preach, and to say, Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (Matthew 4:17). Matthew records Jesus “preaching the gospel of the kingdom” in the cities and villages (9:35). Mark records “preaching the gospel of the kingdom of God” (1:14), and Luke records, “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (4:43). The sending of the Twelve was to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (Matthew 10:6). The comparisons are spoken to people in that ministry.'))
A(('ctbl', 'The verses that set the scene are laid out below.', [
    ['Verse', 'KJV text', 'What to observe'],
    ['Matt 9:9–11', '“Follow me”; “Why eateth your Master with publicans and sinners?”', 'The call, the feast, the Pharisees’ question.'],
    ['Matt 9:12–13', '“They that be whole need not a physician, but they that are sick”', 'Jesus answers the Pharisees.'],
    ['Matt 9:14', '“Why do we and the Pharisees fast oft, but thy disciples fast not?”', 'The question about fasting.'],
    ['Matt 9:15', '“the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken from them”', 'The saying that comes just before the cloth.'],
    ['Mark 2:18', '“Why do the disciples of John and of the Pharisees fast, but thy disciples fast not?”', 'The same question in Mark.'],
    ['Luke 5:33', '“Why do the disciples of John fast often, and make prayers”', 'The same question in Luke.'],
    ['Matt 4:17; 9:35', '“Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand”', 'What was preached, and to whom.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The comparisons are read here in the order each gospel gives them. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another passage, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it. The KJV word is “bottles,” and the quotations keep it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 Matthew’s cloth (9:16). “No man putteth a piece of new cloth unto an old garment, for that which is put in to fill it up taketh from the garment, and the rent is made worse.” The sequence has four steps. No man puts a piece of new cloth on an old garment. The piece is “put in to fill it up.” It “taketh from the garment.” “The rent is made worse.”'))
A(('p', '3.2 Matthew’s wine (9:17). “Neither do men put new wine into old bottles: else the bottles break, and the wine runneth out, and the bottles perish: but they put new wine into new bottles, and both are preserved.” Men do not put new wine into old bottles. If they do, three things follow: the bottles break, the wine runs out, and the bottles perish. The opposite is stated with “but”: they put new wine into new bottles, and “both are preserved.”'))
A(('p', '3.3 Mark’s cloth (2:21). “No man also seweth a piece of new cloth on an old garment: else the new piece that filled it up taketh away from the old, and the rent is made worse.” Mark has “seweth,” “the new piece that filled it up” and “taketh away from the old.” The result is the same as in Matthew: “the rent is made worse.”'))
A(('p', '3.4 Mark’s wine (2:22). “And no man putteth new wine into old bottles: else the new wine doth burst the bottles, and the wine is spilled, and the bottles will be marred: but new wine must be put into new bottles.” Mark names the new wine as the one that bursts the bottles, and he lists three results: the bottles are burst, the wine is spilled, the bottles will be marred. His closing statement is “new wine must be put into new bottles.” Mark does not add “both are preserved.”'))
A(('p', '3.5 Luke’s cloth (5:36). “And he spake also a parable unto them; No man putteth a piece of a new garment upon an old; if otherwise, then both the new maketh a rent, and the piece that was taken out of the new agreeth not with the old.” Luke names “a piece of a new garment,” not new cloth. The new piece is “taken out of the new,” and two results follow: “the new maketh a rent,” and the piece “agreeth not with the old.”'))
A(('p', '3.6 Luke’s wine (5:37–38). “And no man putteth new wine into old bottles; else the new wine will burst the bottles, and be spilled, and the bottles shall perish. But new wine must be put into new bottles; and both are preserved.” Luke has the bursting, the spilling and the perishing, then the statement that new wine “must be put into new bottles,” and “both are preserved.”'))
A(('p', '3.7 Luke’s old wine (5:39). “No man also having drunk old wine straightway desireth new: for he saith, The old is better.” This verse is in Luke only. It says that a man who has drunk old wine does not “straightway” desire new, and it gives his reason in his own words: “The old is better.” The text does not say whether Jesus agrees with that saying, and it does not say who the man is.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 9:16', '“a piece of new cloth unto an old garment”', 'New cloth; the rent is made worse.'],
    ['Matt 9:17', '“put new wine into new bottles, and both are preserved”', 'Both are preserved.'],
    ['Mark 2:21', '“the new piece that filled it up taketh away from the old”', 'The same result in Mark’s words.'],
    ['Mark 2:22', '“new wine must be put into new bottles”', 'The closing statement; no “both are preserved.”'],
    ['Luke 5:36', '“the piece that was taken out of the new agreeth not with the old”', 'The piece does not agree with the old.'],
    ['Luke 5:39', '“The old is better”', 'In Luke only; a man’s own saying.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '3.8 What the stories contain. The cloth story has five things that can be counted in the text: a man, a piece of new cloth, an old garment, a rent and a making worse. The wine story has a man or men, new wine, old bottles, new bottles, a bursting or breaking, a spilling and a preserving. Luke adds a man who has drunk old wine and his saying.'))
A(('p', '3.9 What the stories leave unsaid. Jesus does not say who the man is who puts the piece on the garment or the wine into the bottles. Jesus does not say what the new cloth or the old garment is. Jesus does not say what the new wine or the old wine is. Jesus does not say what the old bottles or the new bottles are. Jesus does not say who “men” are in Matthew 9:17. Jesus does not say whether the man who has drunk old wine is right. The stories stop at the loss, the preserving or the saying, and they say no more.'))
